Tritone Tidefall
Ingredients: SYNTHPOP & NEW ROMANTICS + CINEMATIC, EPIC & ORCHESTRAL + Steel Drums + 2020s ERA
Creative Catalyst: The genre was born at an illegal rooftop rave in a flooded megacity, 2091.

Sound Description
A breathtaking, water-logged collision of weeping New Romantic tenor vocals and colossal, screen-shattering orchestral brass, anchored by the impossible, shimmering acoustic weight of submerged steel drums. Melancholic 1980s analog synthesizers bleed rust and saltwater, while 2020s trap-adjacent vocal processing wrestles with 90-piece string sections that swell like rogue storm surges against concrete pillars.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 94 to 108 BPM, featuring a syncopated, wading rhythm that mimics boots splashing through knee-high rooftop puddles.
- Harmonic Palette: Modal mixtures of Lydian and Minor Pentatonic, utilizing watery chorused chord progressions played on vintage polysynths.
- Production Signatures: Hydro-filtered compression that makes every steel drum note sound like it is echoing inside a sunken cathedral; sidechained French horns; tape-wowed 1980s vocal harmonies.
- Dynamics: Monumental, cinematic swells that drop instantly into claustrophobic, dripping bedroom-pop intimacy, mimicking the sudden arrival of flash floods.

Scene & Origin Lore
In 2091, as the Pacific swallowed the lower tiers of the metropolis, survivors gathered on the high-rise skeletal frames of abandoned skyscrapers, rigging stolen generators to floating amplifier rafts. They wore water-stained silk trench coats, tattered lace Jabots, and neon-lit scuba harnesses. Their rituals involved submerging steel drum pans in rainwater basins to alter their metallic resonance, creating an acoustic hybrid of high-society romanticism and aquatic desperation.

Instruments
Custom water-submerged Steel Drums, Roland Jupiter-8 polysynths, French Horns, 90-piece Orchestral String Sections, 808 sub-bass, and distressed 2020s Vocal Processors.

